{"name":"Coquina","hex":"#9d8d8e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coquina","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several small marine clams, of the species Donax variabilis, common in United States coastal waters. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A soft form of limestone made of fragments of shells, sometimes used as a building or road paving material."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coquina"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coquina","blocks":[{"text":"Coquina is a sedimentary rock that is composed either wholly or almost entirely of the transported, abraded, and mechanically sorted fragments of mollusks, trilobites, brachiopods, or other invertebrates. The term coquina comes from the Spanish word for \"cockle\" and \"shellfish\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Marine_terrace_64_%28Quaternary_-_Coquimbo_Fm.%29.jpg/330px-Marine_terrace_64_%28Quaternary_-_Coquimbo_Fm.%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coquina","wikidata":"Q1225001"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg/500px-Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg/1280px-Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg/500px-Washington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coquina outcrop on the beach at Washington Oaks State Gardens, Florida","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWashington_Oaks_Gardens_coquina01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipe